# Dungeon Dockerfile Procedure

## 1. Purpose

This document describes the Dockerfile of the **Dungeon** backend (ASP.NET Core, .NET 10) and the hardening choices made for it.

Goals:

- Produce a small, production-only image (no SDK, no source code, no tests)
- Run the application as a **non-root** user
- Keep builds fast thanks to Docker layer caching and a NuGet cache
- Keep credentials for private NuGet feeds **out of the image**
- Allow CI to run the unit tests with the same Dockerfile
- Use the same structure as the other .NET microservices (e.g. Leaderboard)

## 2. Solution layout

```
NuGet.config
Dungeon.Presentation.slnx
Dungeon.Contracts/        Dungeon.Contracts.csproj
Dungeon.Domain/           Dungeon.Domain.csproj
Dungeon.Application/      Dungeon.Application.csproj
Dungeon.Infrastructure/   Dungeon.Infrastructure.csproj
Dungeon.Presentation/     Dungeon.Presentation.csproj   <- ASP.NET Core entry point
Dungeon.Test/             Dungeon.Test.csproj
```

## 3. Build pipeline

The Dockerfile is a multi-stage build with five stages:

```
restore --> build --+--> test
                    |
                    +--> publish --> runtime (final image)
```

| Stage | Base image | Role |
|-------|------------|------|
| `restore` | `dotnet/sdk:10.0` | Restores NuGet packages from the project files only |
| `build` | `restore` | Copies the full source and compiles `Dungeon.Presentation` in Release |
| `test` | `build` | Runs the unit tests (`Dungeon.Test`). Only built on demand |
| `publish` | `build` | Produces the deployable files in `/app/publish` |
| `runtime` | `dotnet/aspnet:10.0` | Final image: runtime + published files only |

Only the `runtime` stage ends up in the delivered image.

## 4. Stage details

### 4.1 Syntax and version

```dockerfile
# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.10
ARG DOTNET_VERSION=10.0
```

Dockerfile syntax `1.10` is required for the `env=` option of secret mounts (see 4.2). The .NET version is a single build argument shared by the SDK and runtime images.

### 4.2 Restore

```dockerfile
COPY NuGet.config ./
COPY Dungeon.Presentation.slnx ./
COPY Dungeon.Domain/Dungeon.Domain.csproj Dungeon.Domain/
# ... one COPY per .csproj ...

RUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.nuget/packages \
    --mount=type=secret,id=github_packages_credentials,env=NuGetPackageSourceCredentials_github \
    dotnet restore Dungeon.Presentation.slnx
```

- **Only `.csproj` files, the solution and `NuGet.config` are copied first.** If only C# code changes, Docker reuses the restore layer and does not download packages again.
- **BuildKit cache mount** on `/root/.nuget/packages` speeds up restores across builds.
- **Secret mount** gives `dotnet restore` access to the private GitHub Packages feed. The credentials are injected as the environment variable `NuGetPackageSourceCredentials_github` for this single command only: they are **not** stored in any image layer or in the build history. The suffix `github` must match the package source name declared in `NuGet.config`.

### 4.3 Build

```dockerfile
FROM restore AS build
COPY . .
RUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.nuget/packages \
    dotnet build Dungeon.Presentation/Dungeon.Presentation.csproj \
    --configuration Release --no-restore
```

The full source is copied here, after the restore, so source changes only invalidate this stage and the following ones. `--no-restore` avoids restoring a second time.

### 4.4 Test

```dockerfile
FROM build AS test
RUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.nuget/packages \
    dotnet test Dungeon.Test/Dungeon.Test.csproj \
    --configuration Release --no-restore \
    --verbosity normal --logger "console;verbosity=normal"
```

The build stage only compiles `Dungeon.Presentation`, so `dotnet test` compiles the test project itself (there is no `--no-build`). If a test fails, the Docker build exits with a non-zero code and CI stops.

This stage is not part of the default build. It is only run with `--target test`.

### 4.5 Publish

```dockerfile
FROM build AS publish
RUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.nuget/packages \
    dotnet publish Dungeon.Presentation/Dungeon.Presentation.csproj \
    --configuration Release --output /app/publish \
    --no-build --no-restore /p:UseAppHost=false
```

`/p:UseAppHost=false` skips the native launcher executable, because the container starts the application with `dotnet Dungeon.Presentation.dll`.

### 4.6 Runtime

```dockerfile
FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:${DOTNET_VERSION} AS runtime
WORKDIR /app

ENV ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production \
    ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS=8080 \
    DOTNET_RUNNING_IN_CONTAINER=true \
    DOTNET_EnableDiagnostics=0

EXPOSE 8080 8081

COPY --from=publish --chown=$APP_UID:$APP_UID /app/publish ./
USER $APP_UID
ENTRYPOINT ["dotnet", "Dungeon.Presentation.dll"]
```

| Setting | Why |
|---------|-----|
| `aspnet` image | Runtime only: no SDK, compiler or source code |
| `ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production` | Production configuration and error handling |
| `ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS=8080` | Listens on an unprivileged port (required for non-root) |
| `DOTNET_EnableDiagnostics=0` | Disables diagnostic endpoints/tooling in production |
| `--chown=$APP_UID` + `USER $APP_UID` | Files owned by, and process run as, the unprivileged `app` user provided by the Microsoft image |

## 5. Security summary

- Multi-stage build: the final image contains neither the SDK, the source code, nor the test project
- Non-root execution
- Private NuGet credentials injected through a BuildKit secret, never persisted in the image
- Diagnostics disabled in production
- Nothing sensitive in the build context (use a `.dockerignore` excluding `.git`, `bin/`, `obj/`, `.env*`, keys and certificates)

## 6. How to build and run

The private feed credentials are passed as a secret. The value follows the NuGet format `Username=<user>;Password=<token>`.

```bash
export GITHUB_PACKAGES_CREDENTIALS='Username=<user>;Password=<token>'

# Run the tests
docker build --target test \
  --secret id=github_packages_credentials,env=GITHUB_PACKAGES_CREDENTIALS \
  -t dungeon-test .

# Build the production image
docker build \
  --secret id=github_packages_credentials,env=GITHUB_PACKAGES_CREDENTIALS \
  -t dungeon-backend:local .

# Run it
docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 dungeon-backend:local
```

In CI, the image is built and published by the CD workflow (see `Dungeon_CD_Procedure.md`).

## 7. Possible next improvements

- Switch the runtime image to the **chiseled** variant (`aspnet:10.0-noble-chiseled`): no shell or package manager, smaller attack surface. Health checks must then rely on Kubernetes probes instead of in-container commands.
- **Pin base images by digest** (`@sha256:...`) for supply-chain integrity, and update them deliberately.
- Scan the final image for vulnerabilities in CI (Trivy, Grype or Docker Scout).
- Clarify the ports: the application listens on `8080` (`ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS`), while `EXPOSE` also declares `8081`. `EXPOSE` is documentation only; if a second endpoint is really used, it must also be configured (for example `ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS=8080;8081`), otherwise `8081` should be removed.
